3.5 What a lockfile actually pins
Both lockfiles are plain text, and that’s the point. Open one and it reads like a shopping list:

uv.lock (via uvr) records each R/Python package with exact version + hashes; renv.lock records each R package + version + source. Neither stores the packages, only the recipe to rebuild them; uvr adds the pinned R version on top.



# uvr / uv.lock (excerpt) [[package]] name = “dplyr” version = “1.1.4” hash = “sha256:2f3a9…”
# and R itself is pinned too requires-python = “&gt;=4.6.1”


# renv.lock (excerpt) “dplyr”: { “Version”: “1.1.4”, “Source”: “CRAN” }



A lockfile isn’t magic; it’s an explicit, machine-readable record of what you ran with. Diff it over time to see exactly when an environment changed.


markdown## What a lockfile actually pins {.inv}

Both lockfiles are plain text, and that's the point. Open one and it reads like a shopping list:

::: {.callout-desc}
`uv.lock` (via uvr) records each R/Python package with **exact version + hashes**; `renv.lock` records each R package + version + source. Neither stores the packages, only the recipe to rebuild them; `uvr` adds the **pinned R version** on top.
:::

::: {.split-2}
::: {.term}
&lt;span style="color:rgba(241,238,230,0.45);"&gt;# uvr / uv.lock (excerpt)&lt;/span&gt;
[[package]]
name = "dplyr"
version = "1.1.4"
hash = "sha256:2f3a9..."

&lt;span style="color:rgba(241,238,230,0.45);"&gt;# and R itself is pinned too&lt;/span&gt;
requires-python = "&gt;=4.6.1"
:::

::: {.term}
&lt;span style="color:rgba(241,238,230,0.45);"&gt;# renv.lock (excerpt)&lt;/span&gt;
"dplyr": {
  "Version": "1.1.4",
  "Source": "CRAN"
}
:::
:::

::: {.callout-desc}
A lockfile isn't magic; it's an explicit, machine-readable record of what you ran with. Diff it over time to see exactly when an environment changed.
:::

