2.1 The tidyverse at a glance
A family of R packages that work as one: consistent syntax, a shared pipe, and the same data frame in → data frame out flow.

readr: bring data in (read_csv);
tibble: the modern data frame;
dplyr: transform rows, columns, groups;
tidyr: reshape: keep data tidy (pivot_*);
ggplot2: visualise;
type-focused: purrr (iterate), stringr (strings), forcats (factors), lubridate (dates).


Today we master dplyr + tidyr: the transform engine that feeds every ggplot and every analysis. The long tail (purrr, stringr, …) you pick up on demand.


markdown## The tidyverse at a glance {.inv}

A **family of R packages** that work as one: consistent syntax, a shared pipe, and the same *data frame in → data frame out* flow.

- [readr]{.green}: bring data *in* (`read_csv`);
- [tibble]{.green}: the modern data frame;
- [dplyr]{.amber}: *transform* rows, columns, groups;
- [tidyr]{.amber}: *reshape*: keep data [tidy]{.amber} (`pivot_*`);
- [ggplot2]{.indigo}: *visualise*;
- type-focused: `purrr` (iterate), `stringr` (strings), `forcats` (factors), `lubridate` (dates).

::: {.callout-desc}
**Today we master `dplyr` + `tidyr`**: the transform engine that feeds every `ggplot` and every analysis. The long tail (`purrr`, `stringr`, …) you pick up on demand.
:::

2.4 New columns: mutate

mutate.R

penguins |&gt;
  # compute the body-mass index (BMI) in kg/m²
  dplyr::mutate(bmi = body_mass_g / (flipper_length_mm / 100)^2) |&gt;
  # keep only the columns we want to see
  dplyr::select(species, flipper_length_mm, body_mass_g, bmi) |&gt; 
  # show first 5 rows
  dplyr::slice(1:5)

# A tibble: 5 × 4
  species flipper_length_mm body_mass_g   bmi
  &lt;chr&gt;               &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Adelie                181        3750 1145.
2 Adelie                186        3800 1098.
3 Adelie                195        3250  855.
4 Adelie                 NA          NA   NA 
5 Adelie                193        3450  926.





mutate() computes new columns from existing ones, keeping everything else;
New columns are available to later steps in the same pipe;
You can mutate() several columns at once.



markdown## New columns: `mutate` {.inv}

::: {.codewindow .r width="100%"}
mutate.R
```{r}
#| echo: true
penguins |&gt;
  # compute the body-mass index (BMI) in kg/m²
  dplyr::mutate(bmi = body_mass_g / (flipper_length_mm / 100)^2) |&gt;
  # keep only the columns we want to see
  dplyr::select(species, flipper_length_mm, body_mass_g, bmi) |&gt; 
  # show first 5 rows
  dplyr::slice(1:5)
```
:::

::: {.paper-card}
- [mutate()](https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/reference/mutate.html) computes **new columns** from existing ones, keeping everything else;
- New columns are available to later steps in the **same** pipe;
- You can `mutate()` several columns at once.
:::

2.5 Recode with case_when

case-when.R


penguins |&gt;
  # create a new column "size" based on body mass
  dplyr::mutate(size = dplyr::case_when(
    body_mass_g &gt;= 5000 ~ "large",
    body_mass_g &gt;= 3500 ~ "medium",
    .default = "small"
  )) |&gt; 
  # keep only the columns we want to see
  dplyr::select(species, body_mass_g, size) |&gt;
  # show first 10 rows
  dplyr::slice(1:10)


# A tibble: 10 × 3
   species body_mass_g size  
   &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt; 
 1 Adelie         3750 medium
 2 Adelie         3800 medium
 3 Adelie         3250 small 
 4 Adelie           NA small 
 5 Adelie         3450 small 
 6 Adelie         3650 medium
 7 Adelie         3625 medium
 8 Adelie         4675 medium
 9 Adelie         3475 small 
10 Adelie         4250 medium





case_when() turns a sequence of if…else conditions into a new column: a tidyverse-friendly replacement for nested ifelse;
case_when() reads like a look-up table: each condition ~ value row is evaluated in order, and .default catches everything else;
Perfect for binning or relabelling inside a mutate().



markdown## Recode with `case_when` {.inv}

::: {.codewindow .r width="100%"}
case-when.R
```{r}
#| echo: true
#| output-location: column
penguins |&gt;
  # create a new column "size" based on body mass
  dplyr::mutate(size = dplyr::case_when(
    body_mass_g &gt;= 5000 ~ "large",
    body_mass_g &gt;= 3500 ~ "medium",
    .default = "small"
  )) |&gt; 
  # keep only the columns we want to see
  dplyr::select(species, body_mass_g, size) |&gt;
  # show first 10 rows
  dplyr::slice(1:10)
```
:::

::: {.paper-card}
- [case_when()](https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/reference/case-and-replace-when.html) turns a sequence of **if…else** conditions into a new column: a tidyverse-friendly replacement for nested `ifelse`;
- `case_when()` reads like a **look-up table**: each `condition ~ value` row is evaluated in order, and `.default` catches everything else;
- Perfect for binning or relabelling inside a `mutate()`.
:::

