markdown## Strings with [{stringr}](https://stringr.tidyverse.org) {.inv}

::: {.codewindow}

::: {.editor .r name="01-string-manipulation.R"}
```{r}
#| echo: true
#| output-location: column
penguins |&gt;
  # species contains "inst" (Chinstrap…)
  dplyr::filter(stringr::str_detect(species, "inst")) |&gt; 
  dplyr::select(species, island, body_mass_g) |&gt;
  dplyr::slice(1:14)
```
:::

::: {.editor .r .fragment name="02-string-manipulation.R"}
```{r}
#| echo: true
#| output-location: column
species_clean &lt;- penguins |&gt;
  # "adelie" → "Adelie"
  dplyr::mutate(
    species = stringr::str_to_title(species)
  )
species_clean |&gt;
  # "Adelie" → "Adélie"
  dplyr::mutate(
    species = stringr::str_replace(
      species, 
      pattern = "Adelie", 
      replacement = "Adélie"
    )
  ) |&gt;
  dplyr::select(species, island, body_mass_g) |&gt;
  dplyr::slice(1:14)
```
:::

:::

::: {.paper-card}
- [{stringr}](https://stringr.tidyverse.org) gives you one consistent family for **text**: every function is `str_*` with the string first, so they all pipe;
- `str_detect` pairs with `filter`,
- `str_replace`/`str_to_*` with `mutate`: strings behave exactly like any tidyverse column once you use `str_*`.
:::

