2.9 across(): apply to many columns

across.R

penguins |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(species) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(dplyr::across(
    .cols = dplyr::where(is.numeric), 
    .fns = \(.x) {
      mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE)
    }
  ))

# A tibble: 3 × 6
  species   bill_length_mm bill_depth_mm flipper_length_mm body_mass_g  year
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Adelie              38.8          18.3              190.       3701. 2008.
2 Chinstrap           48.8          18.4              196.       3733. 2008.
3 Gentoo              47.5          15.0              217.       5076. 2008.





across() applies one expression to every column that matches a selector: no copy-pasting verbs per column;
across(where(is.numeric), ...) targets a whole family of columns at once: the bridge from “many similar lines” to programmatic thinking you’ll meet again in Session 3’s functions;
\() {} is the anonymous (lambda) function syntax to create a custom function.



markdown## `across()`: apply to many columns {.inv}

::: {.codewindow .r width="100%"}
across.R
```{r}
#| echo: true
penguins |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(species) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(dplyr::across(
    .cols = dplyr::where(is.numeric), 
    .fns = \(.x) {
      mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE)
    }
  ))
```
:::

::: {.paper-card}
- [`across()`](https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/reference/across.html) applies one expression to **every column that matches** a selector: no copy-pasting verbs per column;
- `across(where(is.numeric), ...)` targets a whole **family** of columns at once: the bridge from "many similar lines" to programmatic thinking you'll meet again in Session 3's functions;
- `\() {}` is the anonymous (lambda) function syntax to create a custom function.
:::

